==Old English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|ang|haldan}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|gem-pro|ang}} {{term/t|gem-pro|*haldaną||to watch, look after}}.  Cognate with Old Frisian ''[[halda]]'' (West Frisian {{term|hâlde|lang=fy}}), Old Saxon {{term|haldan|lang=osx}} (Low German {{term|holen|lang=nds}}), Old Dutch ''[[haldan]]'' (Dutch ''[[houden]]''), Old High German ''[[haltan]]'' (German ''[[halten]]''), Old Norse ''[[halda]]'' (Swedish ''[[hålla]]'', Danish {{term|holde|lang=da}}), Gothic {{term|𐌷𐌰𐌻𐌳𐌰𐌽|tr=haldan|lang=got}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈhæɑldɑn/|lang=ang}}

===Verb===
{{ang-verb}}

# to keep watch over (cattle etc.)
# to [[hold]] fast, to [[grasp]]
# to [[contain]]
# to [[possess]]
# {{context|intransitive|lang=ang}} to maintain one’s position against an enemy

